  "executive_briefing": "In mid-2026, the global corporate tax and trade environment is undergoing a deep structural shift. The OECD Pillar Two 15% global minimum tax is now fully operational across approximately 140 jurisdictions, altering multi-national financial planning and prompting international tax rate adjustments. To maintain competitiveness under these new minimum tax rules, ASEAN hubs like Singapore are holding statutory corporate income tax rates at 17% while deploying aggressive offsetting mechanisms, such as targeted corporate income tax rebates and grants. Concurrently, international trade is marked by high friction, with unilateral tariff updates and executive litigation causing supply chain uncertainty across North America, Europe, and Asia. Meanwhile, digital indirect tax enforcement is accelerating globally, led by continuous transaction controls and standardized Peppol e-invoicing regimes\u2014such as Singapore's phased InvoiceNow initiative running through 2031\u2014redefining day-to-day corporate compliance and audit readiness.",
